## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to turn path-based objectives into simple local transitions without extraneous state. The incremental gain depends only on adjacent cell values, enabling a clean DP.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with monotone moves
- Backpointers for path reconstruction
- Space optimization potential

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxAscents(grid){
  const n=grid.length, m=grid[0].length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(0));
  const par=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(null));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<m;j++){
      if(i===0 && j===0) continue;
      let top=-Infinity, left=-Infinity;
      if(i>0) top = dp[i-1][j] + (grid[i-1][j] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(j>0) left = dp[i][j-1] + (grid[i][j-1] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(top >= left){ dp[i][j] = top; par[i][j] = 'U'; } else { dp[i][j] = left; par[i][j] = 'L'; }
    }
  }
  // backtrack to build path
  return {score: dp[n-1][m-1], path: []};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests your ability to structure a dynamic programming solution for exact-k resource selection with non-overlapping intervals. It requires combining weighted interval scheduling with a prefix DP over both task count and index, along with binary search for efficient predecessor lookup and clean reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with exact k constraint
- Prefix DP over tasks (i) and count (t)
- Precomputing p[i] via binary search for O(log n) lookups
- Backtracking through DP table for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function weightedIntervalScheduling(tasks, K) {
  // Sort by end time
  tasks.sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  const n = tasks.length;

  // Precompute p[i] - last non-overlapping task
  const ends = tasks.map(t => t.end);
  const p = new Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let lo = 0, hi = i - 1, res = -1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = Math.floor) / 2((lo + hi);
      if (tasks[mid].end <= tasks[i].start) {
        res = mid;
        lo = mid + 1;
      } else {
        hi = mid - 1;
      }
    }
    p[i] = res;
  }

  // DP table: dp[i][t] = max value using first i tasks for t selections
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(K + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= K; t++) {
      // Skip task i-1
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      // Take task i-1
      if (p[i - 1] !== -1) {
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[p[i - 1] + 1][t - 1] + tasks[i - 1].value);
      } else if (t === 1) {
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], tasks[i - 1].value);
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const chosen = [];
  let i = n, t = K;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i - 1][t]) {
      i--;
    } else {
      chosen.push(i - 1);
      const prev = p[i - 1];
      i = prev + 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][K], chosenTasks: chosen.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine subarray maxima with partition DP, requiring both maximization and reconstruction of multiple intervals. It exercises careful indexing and boundary handling.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays with a cap k
- Precomputation of maxSub for O(1) subarray maxima
- 2D DP with reconstruction pointers
- Time/space tradeoffs and edge cases (k=0, equal values)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxSub = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, A[r]);
      maxSub[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let best = dp[i-1][t];
      let bestJ = -1;
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = maxSub[j-1][i-1] + dp[j-1][t-1];
        if (val > best) { best = val; bestJ = j; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = best; take[i][t] = bestJ;
    }
  }
  const res = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    const j = take[i][t];
    if (j === -1) break;
    res.push([j-1, i-1]);
    i = j - 1;
    t--;
  }
  res.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], subarrays: res};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests translating a DP state into a practical recurrence with non-overlap constraint and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over index i and count t
- Non-overlapping constraint handled by dp[i-2] for taking a domino
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const NEG=-1e15;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=2;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      const v1=dp[i-1][t];
      const v2=dp[i-2][t-1]+A[i-2]+A[i-1];
      if(v2>v1){ dp[i][t]=v2; take[i][t]=true; }
      else dp[i][t]=v1;
    }
  }
  // backtrack
  // ... reconstruct domino starts from i=n, t=k
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

